## 🔹 ECS Launch Types

I learned about two important ways of running ECS workloads:

### 1. ECS with EC2

With the EC2 launch type, we manage the underlying EC2 instances.

```text
ECS Cluster
     ↓
EC2 Instances
     ↓
ECS Tasks
     ↓
Containers
```

This gives more control over the underlying infrastructure, but we are also responsible for managing the EC2 capacity.

### 2. AWS Fargate

With **Fargate**, AWS manages the underlying server infrastructure for the containers.

```text
ECS
 ↓
Fargate
 ↓
Task
 ↓
Container
```

We mainly focus on defining the task requirements rather than managing EC2 instances ourselves.

---

## 🔹 ECS Networking

ECS tasks running with the appropriate networking configuration can be connected to an AWS VPC.

I learned about concepts such as:

* VPC
* Subnets
* Security Groups
* Task networking
* Container ports

For example:

```text
Internet
    ↓
Load Balancer
    ↓
ECS Service
    ↓
ECS Tasks
    ↓
Containers
```

Security Groups can control which traffic is allowed to reach the application.

---

## 🔹 ECS + Application Load Balancer

For web applications, an **Application Load Balancer (ALB)** can be placed in front of an ECS service.

For example:

```text
User
 ↓
Application Load Balancer
 ↓
ECS Service
 ↓
Task 1
Task 2
Task 3
```

The load balancer distributes incoming traffic across healthy tasks.

This allows multiple container instances to serve the same application.

---

## 🔹 ECS Scaling

ECS services can run multiple copies of a task.

For example:

```text
Low traffic
    ↓
2 Tasks

High traffic
    ↓
5 Tasks
```

This allows applications to handle changing workloads.

ECS can work with AWS scaling capabilities to automatically adjust the number of running tasks based on configured conditions.

---

## 🔹 ECS vs Docker

Docker is mainly used to:

```text
Build
Run
Package
Ship
```

containers.

ECS is used to:

```text
Deploy
Manage
Scale
Monitor
```

containers across AWS infrastructure.

So they work together rather than being direct replacements for each other.

```text
Docker
   ↓
Build Image
   ↓
ECR
   ↓
ECS
   ↓
Run & Manage Containers
```

---

## 🔹 ECS vs Kubernetes

I also connected ECS with my existing Kubernetes knowledge.

The concepts are roughly comparable:

| ECS             | Kubernetes                                  |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| ECS Cluster     | Kubernetes Cluster                          |
| Task Definition | Pod specification / workload configuration  |
| Task            | Pod                                         |
| ECS Service     | Kubernetes Deployment/Service concepts      |
| ECR             | Container Registry                          |
| Fargate         | Managed/serverless container compute option |

However, ECS is an **AWS-native container orchestration service**, while Kubernetes is an open-source container orchestration platform that can be managed through services such as **Amazon EKS**.

